# AI on the phone

## Overview

This notebook climbs a ladder: from plain tensor maths to three
machine-learning models of increasing size, all running on the phone's
CPU with no GPU or cloud service. It builds on the earlier notebooks:
Wi-Fi (01), the camera (05) and audio (10).

## The layers

```mermaid
graph TD
  nerves_ai["nerves_ai<br/>meta-package: wires backends at boot"]
  nerves_ai --> infer_llm & infer_vision & infer_audio & nx_primitives
  nerves_ai --> cpu_governor & nerves_model_hub & nerves_data_resize
  infer_llm["infer_llm<br/>Whisper, LLM helpers"] --> arm_ai
  infer_vision["infer_vision<br/>YOLO, ONNX models"] --> arm_ai
  infer_audio["infer_audio<br/>decode, resample, WAV"] --> arm_ai
  nx_primitives["nx_primitives<br/>FFT, embeddings, int8"] --> arm_ai
  nx_arm["nx_arm<br/>Nx backend on NEON"] --> arm_ai
  arm_ai["arm_ai<br/>Rust NIF: NEON kernels, candle, tract"]
```

From the bottom up:

* **`arm_ai`** is the engine: a Rust library loaded into the BEAM as a
  NIF. It has NEON-optimised maths kernels, runs language models and
  Whisper with [candle](https://github.com/huggingface/candle) and ONNX
  models with [tract](https://github.com/sonos/tract), and decodes
  images and audio.
* **`nx_arm`** plugs `arm_ai` into [Nx](https://hexdocs.pm/nx), Elixir's
  tensor library, as a backend.
* **`nx_primitives`**, **`infer_llm`**, **`infer_vision`** and
  **`infer_audio`** are generic Elixir libraries: each defines what it
  needs as a behaviour (`<Library>.Backend`), and `arm_ai` provides the
  ARM implementation. Another backend could run the same code elsewhere.
* **`cpu_governor`**, **`nerves_model_hub`** and **`nerves_data_resize`**
  are Nerves helpers for CPU frequency, model downloads and disk space.
* **`nerves_ai`** depends on all of them and, at boot, tells each generic
  library to use the `arm_ai` backend.

## The repositories

| Library | What it does |
|---|---|
| [`arm_ai`](https://github.com/mlainez/arm_ai) | Rust NIF: NEON kernels, candle LLM and Whisper, tract ONNX, image and audio decoding |
| [`nx_arm`](https://github.com/mlainez/nx_arm) | `Nx.Backend` and `Nx.Defn.Compiler` on NEON |
| [`nx_primitives`](https://github.com/mlainez/nx_primitives) | FFT, embeddings, int8 matrix maths |
| [`infer_llm`](https://github.com/mlainez/infer_llm) | Whisper speech-to-text, sampling, KV cache helpers |
| [`infer_vision`](https://github.com/mlainez/infer_vision) | YOLO object detection, generic ONNX models, image preprocessing |
| [`infer_audio`](https://github.com/mlainez/infer_audio) | Audio decoding, resampling, WAV writing |
| [`cpu_governor`](https://github.com/mlainez/cpu_governor) | big.LITTLE topology, running code at full CPU speed |
| [`nerves_model_hub`](https://github.com/mlainez/nerves_model_hub) | Download models from Hugging Face or any URL, with checksums |
| [`nerves_data_resize`](https://github.com/mlainez/nerves_data_resize) | Grows the data partition on first boot so models fit |
| [`nerves_ai`](https://github.com/mlainez/nerves_ai) | Meta-package that ties the stack together |

The TinyLlama chat at the end calls `ArmAI.LlamaCandle` in `arm_ai` directly.

## The backends on this phone

Each generic library reads its backend from its application config;
`nerves_ai` set these at boot:

```elixir
for app <- [:nx_primitives, :infer_llm, :infer_vision, :infer_audio] do
  {app, Application.get_env(app, :backend)}
end
```


## Nx in one paragraph

`Nx` is Elixir's numerical library: multi-dimensional tensors and the
maths on them, the building block for machine learning in Elixir.
`nx_arm` gives Nx a backend that runs on the phone's ARM NEON vector
unit, across all its cores.

## The CPU

The Snapdragon 632 has two clusters of four Kryo 250 cores ("big" and
"LITTLE"). `cpu_governor` reads the layout from `/sys`:

```elixir
CpuGovernor.topology()
```

## Tensors

Tensors live in a backend. `NxArm.Backend` keeps them as binaries and
computes with NEON-optimised native code.

```elixir
a = Nx.tensor([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]], backend: NxArm.Backend)
b = Nx.tensor([[0.5, 0.0], [0.0, 2.0]], backend: NxArm.Backend)

{Nx.dot(a, b), Nx.exp(a), Nx.sum(a)}
```

## How much faster is NEON?

The same 128×128 matrix multiplication on Nx's pure-Elixir backend and
on NEON. `CpuGovernor.with_performance/1` runs the cores at full speed
for the duration and restores the previous setting afterwards.

```elixir
time_ms = fn fun ->
  {us, _} = :timer.tc(fun)
  Float.round(us / 1000, 1)
end

x = Nx.iota({128, 128}, type: :f32) |> Nx.divide(1000)

CpuGovernor.with_performance(fn ->
  plain = Nx.backend_transfer(x, Nx.BinaryBackend)
  neon = Nx.backend_transfer(x, NxArm.Backend)
  Nx.dot(neon, neon)

  %{
    pure_elixir_ms: time_ms.(fn -> Nx.dot(plain, plain) end),
    neon_ms: time_ms.(fn -> Nx.dot(neon, neon) end)
  }
end)
```

## Models: downloaded when you need them

The next steps use trained models. They're too big for the firmware and
none is downloaded automatically: each step fetches its model the first
time with `NervesModelHub.ensure_one/2`, from Hugging Face (`{:hf, repo,
file}`) or any URL. It checks the SHA-256, writes the file atomically to
`/data/models`, and skips the download when the file is already there.

The phone needs internet access for the first run. If this doesn't say
`:internet`, connect to Wi-Fi with the Wi-Fi notebook:

```elixir
VintageNet.get(["connection"])
```

The model steps below download with this small helper: it returns the
file's path, or stops with a message saying what went wrong.

```elixir
fetch_model = fn id, spec ->
  case NervesModelHub.ensure_one(id, spec) do
    {:ok, path} ->
      path

    {:error, reason} ->
      raise "Couldn't download #{id}: #{inspect(reason)}. " <>
              "Is the phone online? Check with the Wi-Fi notebook."
  end
end
```

## Find objects in a photo (YOLO)

**YOLOv5n** finds and labels objects in a picture: people, chairs,
bottles, laptops and 76 other everyday categories. The model is 4 MB and
comes from Ultralytics' GitHub release; `InferVision.YOLO` runs it.

```elixir
yolo_path =
  fetch_model.(:yolov5n,
    source: {:url, "https://github.com/ultralytics/yolov5/releases/download/v7.0/yolov5n.onnx"},
    path: "/data/models/yolov5n.onnx",
    sha256: "04f0e55c26f58d17145b36045780fe1250d5bd2187543e11568e5141d05b3262"
  )

{:ok, yolo} = InferVision.YOLO.load(yolo_path, decoder: InferVision.YOLO.Decoders.V5)
```

The model answers with class numbers; these are their names (the COCO
dataset it was trained on):

```elixir
labels =
  ~w(person bicycle car motorcycle airplane bus train truck boat traffic_light
     fire_hydrant stop_sign parking_meter bench bird cat dog horse sheep cow
     elephant bear zebra giraffe backpack umbrella handbag tie suitcase frisbee
     skis snowboard sports_ball kite baseball_bat baseball_glove skateboard
     surfboard tennis_racket bottle wine_glass cup fork knife spoon bowl banana
     apple sandwich orange broccoli carrot hot_dog pizza donut cake chair couch
     potted_plant bed dining_table toilet tv laptop mouse remote keyboard
     cell_phone microwave oven toaster sink refrigerator book clock vase
     scissors teddy_bear hair_drier toothbrush)
  |> Enum.map(&String.replace(&1, "_", " "))
  |> List.to_tuple()
```

### Take a photo and detect

Point the back of the phone at something and run the cell. It takes a
2×2-binned photo (quicker than full resolution), scales it to the
640×640 input YOLO expects, and lists what it found.

```elixir
photo = "/tmp/detect.jpg"
IO.puts("Taking the photo...")
{:ok, _} = Fp3Camera.snap(:rear, photo, binned: true)
IO.puts("Looking for objects...")

input =
  InferVision.Preprocess.load_for_classifier(photo,
    size: {640, 640},
    mean: {0.0, 0.0, 0.0},
    std: {1.0, 1.0, 1.0}
  )

detections = InferVision.YOLO.detect(yolo, input)

for d <- detections do
  %{object: elem(labels, d.class), confidence: Float.round(d.score, 2)}
end
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

### See it

Boxes are in the 640×640 input's coordinates; they're scaled back to the
photo and drawn on top of it.

```elixir
{:ok, {_rgb, width, height}} = InferVision.Preprocess.decode_to_rgb8(photo)
sx = width / 640
sy = height / 640

boxes =
  for %{box: {x1, y1, x2, y2}, class: class, score: score} <- detections do
    x = x1 * sx
    y = y1 * sy

    """
    <rect x="#{x}" y="#{y}" width="#{(x2 - x1) * sx}" height="#{(y2 - y1) * sy}"
          fill="none" stroke="yellow" stroke-width="#{width / 250}"/>
    <text x="#{x + 6}" y="#{y + width / 30}" fill="yellow" font-size="#{width / 32}"
          font-family="sans-serif">#{elem(labels, class)} #{round(score * 100)}%</text>
    """
  end

jpeg = Base.encode64(File.read!(photo))

Kino.HTML.new("""
<svg viewBox="0 0 #{width} #{height}" style="max-width: 100%">
  <image href="data:image/jpeg;base64,#{jpeg}" width="#{width}" height="#{height}"/>
  #{Enum.join(boxes)}
</svg>
""")
```

## Speech to text (Whisper)

**Whisper** turns speech into text. This is OpenAI's `tiny.en` model
(English only, about 45 MB), run by `InferLLM.Whisper`.

Whisper needs four files: the weights, the tokenizer and the model
config from the `lmz/candle-whisper` Hugging Face repo, plus the mel
filter bank from the candle repository. `NervesModelHub` fetches each
one once and verifies its SHA-256.

```elixir
repo = "lmz/candle-whisper"

files = %{
  model:
    fetch_model.(:whisper_model,
      source: {:hf, repo, "model-tiny-en-q80.gguf"},
      path: "/data/models/whisper-tiny-en-q80.gguf",
      sha256: "07997efe0b8d3577fc45d9b307ef578a01346f4088a5da4bd92377aaa3f72986"
    ),
  tokenizer:
    fetch_model.(:whisper_tokenizer,
      source: {:hf, repo, "tokenizer-tiny-en.json"},
      path: "/data/models/whisper-tokenizer.json",
      sha256: "5eb60cec1e77aeeb6869a2bb5a8e01a84c3fe5d072d75369343021fe6f5310d0"
    ),
  config:
    fetch_model.(:whisper_config,
      source: {:hf, repo, "config-tiny-en.json"},
      path: "/data/models/whisper-config.json",
      sha256: "b5366317df280f79e6cc366f259f379674f81a8721d458acf2e00680ccb10b1d"
    ),
  mel_filters:
    fetch_model.(:whisper_mel,
      source:
        {:url,
         "https://github.com/huggingface/candle/raw/main/candle-examples/examples/whisper/melfilters.bytes"},
      path: "/data/models/melfilters.bytes",
      sha256: "85818f156f7e189453901a515e4726d270d307f976e161cf9403e8caab405498"
    )
}

{:ok, whisper} =
  InferLLM.Whisper.load(
    model: files.model,
    tokenizer: files.tokenizer,
    config: files.config,
    mel_filters: files.mel_filters
  )
```

### Transcribe a recording

The firmware ships an 11-second excerpt of John F. Kennedy's 1961
inaugural address. Whisper needs 16 kHz mono audio;
`InferAudio.Decoder.load_for_whisper/1` decodes and resamples any WAV
file to that.

```elixir
sample = Application.app_dir(:nerves_livebook_fp3, "priv/audio/jfk.wav")
pcm = InferAudio.Decoder.load_for_whisper(sample)
IO.puts("Transcribing...")
InferLLM.Whisper.transcribe(whisper, pcm)
```

### Transcribe yourself

Records 5 seconds from the phone's main microphone. Speak (in English)
while it runs. The microphone needs a system image whose kernel drives
the WCD9335 audio codec; without it, `arecord` fails here.

```elixir
amixer = fn name, value -> System.cmd("amixer", ["-c0", "cset", "name=#{name}", value]) end
amixer.("MultiMedia2 Mixer SLIMBUS_0_TX", "1")
amixer.("SLIM TX7 MUX", "DEC7")
amixer.("ADC MUX7", "DMIC")
amixer.("DMIC MUX7", "DMIC1")
amixer.("AIF1_CAP Mixer SLIM TX7", "1")

arecord = fn seconds, path ->
  System.cmd("arecord", ~w(-D hw:0,1 -f S16_LE -r 48000 -c 1 -d #{seconds} #{path}),
    stderr_to_stdout: true
  )
end

# The first capture after boot returns silence; a short one wakes the path up.
arecord.(1, "/tmp/warmup.wav")
IO.puts("Recording for 5 seconds: speak now!")
arecord.(5, "/tmp/me.wav")
IO.puts("Transcribing...")

InferLLM.Whisper.transcribe(whisper, InferAudio.Decoder.load_for_whisper("/tmp/me.wav"))
```

## Chat with a language model (TinyLlama)

**TinyLlama 1.1B Chat** is a small language model: about 670 MB, the
biggest step. On a Fairphone 3 it writes about 4.7 tokens per second, so
the answer is shown as it's written.

```elixir
model_path =
  fetch_model.(:tinyllama,
    source: {:hf, "TheBloke/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0-GGUF", "tinyllama-1.1b-chat-v1.0.Q4_K_M.gguf"},
    path: "/data/models/tinyllama.gguf",
    sha256: "9fecc3b3cd76bba89d504f29b616eedf7da85b96540e490ca5824d3f7d2776a0"
  )

tokenizer_path =
  fetch_model.(:tinyllama_tokenizer,
    source: {:hf, "TinyLlama/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0", "tokenizer.json"},
    path: "/data/models/tinyllama-tokenizer.json",
    sha256: "bcd04f0eadf90287bd26e1a183ac487d8a141b09b06aecb7725bbdd343640f2e"
  )
```

### Load the model

Takes a few seconds.

```elixir
{:ok, model} = ArmAI.LlamaCandle.load(model_path, tokenizer: tokenizer_path)
```

### Ask a question

Change `question` and run the cell again.

```elixir
question = "What three things should I see in Brussels?"

prompt = """
<|system|>
You are a helpful, concise travel guide.</s>
<|user|>
#{question}</s>
<|assistant|>
"""

# The answer appears as the model writes it, piece by piece.
for piece <- ArmAI.LlamaCandle.stream(model, prompt: prompt, max_new: 128, stop_tokens: [2]) do
  IO.write(piece)
end

:done
```

`ArmAI.LlamaCandle.generate/2` takes the same options and returns the
whole answer at once, with timing statistics (tokens per second).

`stop_tokens: [2]` ends the answer at TinyLlama's end-of-text token, and
`max_new` caps its length. The `<|system|>`, `<|user|>` and
`<|assistant|>` lines are the chat format TinyLlama was trained on.

## Already on this phone

The models downloaded so far (sizes in MB):

```elixir
case File.ls("/data/models") do
  {:ok, files} ->
    for f <- Enum.sort(files), do: {f, div(File.stat!(Path.join("/data/models", f)).size, 1_000_000)}

  {:error, :enoent} ->
    []
end
```
